WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:09.000
Priya (Product Lead): Morning everyone. This is the Q4 checkout reliability review. We have 45 minutes. Goal is to decide on the payment retry work and lock the November release scope.

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:18.000
Arjun (Engineering Manager): Quick context. Checkout failures went from 1.8% to 3.4% after the gateway migration on the 12th. Most of it is timeouts on UPI collect.

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:31.000
Meera (Support Lead): Support tickets are up 62% this month. The top complaint is "payment stuck" and then people retry manually. We had 214 tickets last week alone.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:44.000
Arjun: Root cause looks like our retry logic hammering the gateway. We send three retries in 400ms. The gateway rate-limits us after that and the collect never resolves.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:58.000
Priya: So the fix is exponential backoff, right?

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:12.000
Arjun: Backoff plus idempotency keys. Right now a retry can double-charge in rare cases. We saw 11 suspected duplicates in the logs. Finance flagged two of them.

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:25.000
Ravi (Finance): Two actual duplicates, both refunded. We need this fixed before Black Friday. If it happens at volume it becomes a compliance issue, not just support.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:40.000
Priya: Agreed. Let's treat double-charge prevention as P0. Arjun, can you own the idempotency work?

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:49.000
Arjun: Yes. I'll need two engineers for about a week. Say by the 6th of November.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:02:05.000
Meera: While that ships, can support get a way to see payment state without asking engineering? Right now every stuck payment is a Slack thread.

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:16.000
Priya: Good point. A read-only payment timeline in the admin panel. Small scope. Meera, write up the fields you need by Friday and we'll size it.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:30.000
Meera: Will do. I'd want gateway response code, retry count, and the last webhook time.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:47.000
Arjun: One more thing. The gateway's sandbox doesn't reproduce the rate limit, so we can't test this properly before production. We'd need their staging keys. That's been pending for three weeks.

00:02:47.500 --> 00:03:02.000
Priya: That's a real blocker. Ravi, can finance push the vendor on staging access? They respond faster to commercial pressure.

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:14.000
Ravi: I'll escalate to our account manager today. If we don't have keys by Wednesday we should consider a canary rollout instead.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:28.000
Priya: Decision: we ship the backoff change behind a feature flag on the 8th, canary at 5% for 48 hours. If error rate doesn't improve by 20% we roll back. Everyone okay?

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:34.000
Arjun: Fine by me.

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:40.000
Ravi: Agreed, but I want a daily failure report during the canary.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:52.000
Priya: Done. Arjun, include the failure rate by gateway in the daily report.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:04:08.000
Meera: Can we also update the customer-facing error message? "Payment failed" is scary. Something like "We're confirming your payment, don't retry for 10 minutes."

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:20.000
Priya: Yes, let's do that. Meera owns the copy, Arjun owns the implementation. Ship with the canary.

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:35.000
Arjun: Note for the record: this touches the checkout service and the notification service. We'll need a coordinated deploy. I'll write the runbook.

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:50.000
Priya: Anything else? ... Okay. Open question: do we delay the BNPL launch? It was planned for the 15th and it depends on the same gateway.

00:04:50.500 --> 00:05:06.000
Ravi: I'd delay it by two weeks. Launching a new payment method on an unstable gateway is asking for trouble.

00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:18.000
Arjun: Agreed, but marketing has already announced it. We need a messaging plan.

00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:29.000
Priya: Let's not decide today. I'll take it to the leadership sync on Monday with a recommendation to move BNPL to the 29th. Marketing comms is a risk either way.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:44.000
Priya: Last item, metrics. How will we know this worked? Arjun, define the success metric and baseline by end of day tomorrow.

00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:58.000
Arjun: Success is checkout failure rate back under 2% and duplicate charges at zero. I'll pull the last 90 days as baseline.

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:10.000
Priya: Great. Next meeting is Thursday the 7th at 10am, same link. Focus is canary results. Please come with numbers, not anecdotes. Thanks everyone.
